    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Create a map\n",
        "\n",
        "### `plot()`\n",
        "\n",
        "Similarly to a `pandas.DataFrame`, a `geopandas.GeoDataFrame` has a `plot()` method that we can call directly to create a quick view of our data. \n",
        "The geospatial information of the `geopandas.GeoDataFrame` will be used to create the axes of the plot.\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Let's take a quick look at our recent pigs data:"
      ],
      "id": "15083443"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "pigs_recent.plot()"
      ],
      "id": "23e800e9",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### `explore()`\n",
        "\n",
        "The `plot()` method creates a static map. \n",
        "To explore our data interactively, we can use the `explore()` method instead. \n",
        "This creates an interactive map where we can zoom in and out, move around, and hover over each feature to see its attributes:"
      ],
      "id": "0fc5c58e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "pigs_recent.explore()"
      ],
      "id": "62b8328b",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### `matplotlib`'s `fig` and `ax`\n",
        "\n",
        "Going forward, we will make more complex visualizations where we add different layers to a graph and customize it. \n",
        "To do this, we will use the `matplotlib` Python library for creating visualizations. \n",
        "We can interact with `matplotlib` via its `pyplot` interface, which we imported at the start of the lesson.\n",
        "\n",
        "Matplotlib graphs the data in a **figure** which can have one or more **axes**. \n",
        "The axes is *only* the area specified by the $x$ axis and $y$ axis and what is plotted in it, while the figure can include multiple axes in it.  \n",
        "\n",
        "![Image source: [Getting Started with Matplotlib.](https://www.skytowner.com/explore/getting_started_with_matplotlib)](/book/images/lesson-10/matplotlib_figure_axes_axis.png)\n",
        "\n",
        "To create a new blank figure:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Initialize a new figure and axes by calling `pyplot`'s `subplots()` function\n",
        "2. Display the figure using `plt.show()`:"
      ],
      "id": "ca8ad7ac"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Initialize empty figure (fig) and axes (ax)\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n",
        "\n",
        "# Display figure\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "9675ffdb",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Notice that `plt.subplots()` is a function that returns two objects. When we call it, we get a figure `fig` with a single empty axes `ax`. We can think of this step as setting a new blank canvas on which we will paint. \n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### Adding a layer\n",
        "\n",
        "When using `matplotlib`, it can be useful to think of creating a plot as adding layers to an axes.\n",
        "The general syntax to plot data from a data frame `df` onto an axes is:\n",
        "```python\n",
        "# Initialize empty figure and axes\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n",
        "\n",
        "# Plot df on the ax axes\n",
        "df.plot(ax=ax,  # Add plot to axes\n",
        "        ...)    # Other arguments for plot function\n",
        "\n",
        "# Display figure\n",
        "plt.show()\n",
        "``` \n",
        "\n",
        "#### Example\n",
        "\n",
        "The first layer that we want to add to our axes is the `pigs_recent` point data. \n",
        "We can plot our data using `matplotlib` like this:"
      ],
      "id": "d37013aa"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Initialize empty figure and axes\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n",
        "\n",
        "# Add pigs point plot to our figure's axes\n",
        "pigs_recent.plot(ax=ax)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Display figure\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "9b49ff62",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Customization \n",
        "\n",
        "Matplotlib allows for a lot of customization. \n",
        "Some of it can be done directly in the `plot()` method for the data frame (like we've done when plotting data using `pandas`), while the rest is done by updating attributes of the `ax` axes. \n",
        "The following diagram shows some examples of elements in the axes that can be updated.\n",
        "\n",
        "![Image source: Matplotlib documentation.](/book/images/lesson-10/anatomy.png){width=\"70%\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Some basic customization for our pigs data could look like this:"
      ],
      "id": "a3074e53"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Initialize empty figure\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n",
        "\n",
        "# Add data to axes\n",
        "pigs_recent.plot(ax=ax,         # Add plot to axes\n",
        "                 alpha=0.5,     # Adjust transparency\n",
        "                 color='brown'  # Update point color\n",
        "                 )\n",
        "\n",
        "# Update axes \n",
        "ax.set_title('Reported \"Sus scrofa\" sightings in CA (2020-2023)')\n",
        "ax.set_xlabel('Longitude')\n",
        "ax.set_ylabel('Latitude')\n",
        "\n",
        "# Display figure\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "0d0c6367",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
